sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/metabolic-scaling-exponent--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-domain heterogeneity test: one exponent across prokaryotes, protists and animals?" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> ;
    dct:description "Universality claims cover all life, but approach 1 tests mammals. DeLong et al. 2010 compiled metabolic rates for prokaryotes, protists and metazoans and reported superlinear, approximately linear and sublinear exponents respectively. Re-analyse with a pre-registered hierarchical model: log B = a_g + b_g log M + temperature correction, with group g ∈ {prokaryotes, protists, ectothermic metazoans, endotherms}, b_g ~ Normal(b̄, τ²). Pre-registered decision: universality is refuted if τ's 95% posterior interval excludes 0.05 and at least two b_g intervals are disjoint; supported if τ < 0.03 and b̄'s interval contains 3/4 or 2/3. Add AnAge ectotherm entries with temperature to test whether temperature correction collapses ectotherm and endotherm slopes, as metabolic theory predicts." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The posterior for between-group slope heterogeneity τ, and whether group slopes overlap. Disjoint slopes across domains refute a single universal exponent regardless of the mammal result." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Group samples (especially prokaryotes and protists) too small or too heterogeneous in measurement protocol for slopes to be compared, leaving τ's interval wide: inconclusive, underpowered." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register the model, priors and decision thresholds. 2. Retrieve DeLong et al. 2010 supplementary data; add AnAge ectotherm records with temperature. 3. Fit the hierarchical model (Stan or PyMC) with measurement-error terms on M where reported. 4. Report τ, group slopes and the verdict; check sensitivity to the temperature correction." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: anage" ;
    skos:note "Tool: bayesian-hierarchical-models" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1007783107> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://genomics.senescence.info/species/dataset.zip> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1007783107> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" .

  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1007783107> dct:title "DeLong et al. 2010 supplementary data" ;
    dct:description "Metabolic rate and mass across prokaryotes, protists and metazoans" .
  <https://genomics.senescence.info/species/dataset.zip> dct:title "AnAge" ;
    dct:description "Ectotherm metabolic rates with temperatures" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1007783107> dct:title "Shifts in metabolic scaling, production, and efficiency across major evolutionary transitions of life" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "DeLong, Okie, Moses, Sibly and Brown 2010, Shifts in metabolic scaling across major evolutionary transitions of life" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997" .
}

